<section class="block"><div class="wrap prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">The gap</div>
  <h2>Family offices back businesses where technology is the part nobody at the table can judge.</h2>
  <p>A family-run office buys a company next to its core industry. A multi-family office takes a seat beside a sponsor. A professionally led office writes a check into a deal that depends on software, data or an AI claim. In each case the demo is polished and the sponsor's own diligence is often the only technical review the deal gets.</p>
  <p>Diligence runs two ways. You are examined by the people raising capital, and you should be examining them. I do the second part, on the technology, and I write it so the whole family can read it, including the member who was not in the room.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="block alt"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="eyebrow">Three ways to work together</div>
  <div class="cols">
    <div><h3>Technical diligence</h3><p>A fixed-scope review of a company's technology, data and AI claims, before you commit. You get a plain-language memo and a call to walk the principals through it.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Advisor on call</h3><p>A monthly retainer. One technical voice you can phone before a decision, who already knows your deals and the sponsors who bring them.</p></div>
    <div><h3>Fractional CTO</h3><p>Hands-on technology leadership inside the company after close, for a short, defined period, with a written plan to hand it to a team that stays.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="block"><div class="wrap prose">
  <h2>What you hand to the family.</h2>
  <p>A document that says what is real, what is a demo, what breaks as the business grows, what it costs to fix, and what to ask the sponsor next. It is written to survive a disagreement inside the family, and to make sense to a lender, a board or the next generation.</p>
